 * The activation, invite and protocol event registrations and unregistrations (`runtime/impl_system.cpp`, tested by `test_system.cpp` and `test_misc.cpp`): a register call rejects a null callback or token with `E_INVALIDARG` (the pages mark the callback required and name no code, like the user registrations above), hands out distinct tokens and never fires offline, and an unregister call answers true: the pages say it returns false when `wait` is false and the unregister is pending, nothing is ever pending here because no callback is ever delivered, and what a token that was never issued does is not documented (true, pinned by `test_misc.cpp`). The current pages mark the protocol and invite pairs deprecated in favour of `XGameActivationRegisterForEvent` and `XGameActivationUnregisterForEvent`, which they say replace both; the stand-in keeps all three pairs, since deprecated does not mean removed and a title may still call them. The `XGameActivationAcceptPendingInvite` page says a successful accept triggers a subsequent activation callback with the `AcceptedGameInvite` type; no invite is ever pending here, so none follows.
 * XStore (`runtime/impl_store.cpp`, tested by `test_store.cpp`): a PC with a cached licence for the running game and no connection to the Store. Contexts are created with or without a user (the documented PC behaviour). The game licence is active, full and permanent, with SKU `<store id>/0010` (the SKU id is a choice); there are no add-on licences. The title's own package can be licensed (`XStoreAcquireLicenseForPackage`), and `XStoreQueryPackageIdentifier` maps its store id to its package identifier; any other package is not owned (`LM_E_CONTENT_NOT_OWNED`), and any other store id has no installed package (the documented `E_INVALIDARG`). Everything that needs the Store service fails with `E_GAMESTORE_NETWORK_ERROR`: entitlement queries (the GDK documents them as needing a connection) and product queries (they read the Store catalogue; their reference pages do not say), `XStoreCanAcquireLicenseFor*` (documented as needing a connection, so it answers the same for the title's own package as for any other), durables, consumables, collections and purchase ids, licence tokens (an empty `customDeveloperString` is `E_INVALIDARG`, documented), package updates and downloads, and the purchase, review, redeem, gifting and product-page UIs. No product query handle is ever returned, so enumerating one is `E_INVALIDARG` and it has no more pages; nothing is purchasable. Licence-changed and licence-lost registrations never fire.
 * Exposed surface (`test_surface.cpp`): the function names the GDK documents (gdk-2610 members pages), by interface. Each must resolve to one slot of its interface in `table.json`, and that slot must be filled in the DLL, by an override or by the logging stub, so a renamed, moved or dropped method shows in CI instead of when a title reaches the slot. Pinned: the table's `XPackageGetKind` is the function the pages call `XPackageGetPackageKind`; `XSystemGetDeviceType` and `XSystemGetRuntimeInfo` are documented for every platform and absent from the table, and the test lists them as absent until they are added. Names the runtime exposes without a reference page (`XTaskQueueIsEmpty`, `XStoreIsAvailabilityPurchasable`, `XAsyncBegin`/`Complete`/`GetResult`/`Schedule`, the three `XGameInvite*Pending*` calls and `XGameActivationAcceptPendingInvite`) are not in the list.
-* Handle tracking (`runtime/impl_handles.cpp`, tested by `test_misc.cpp`): the calls that hand out task queue, user, display timeout deferral, game save provider, container and update, package installation monitor, package mount, store context and store licence handles report them, so `XSystemIsHandleValid` is true from the first reference handed to the title until its last close, and the `XSystemHandleTrack` callback gets Created and Destroyed at those two points (duplicating a handle that returns the same pointer, as `XTaskQueueDuplicateHandle` and `XUserDuplicateHandle` do, adds a reference and no event). The callback runs synchronously on the thread that created or closed the handle; a null callback turns it off, and handles stay tracked for `XSystemIsHandleValid`. `UninitializeApiImpl` (`XGameRuntimeUninitialize`) reports each handle still open through `XErrorReport` with `E_FAIL` and a message naming its type; the code and text are not documented. Not done: handles passed into other calls are not validated (an invalid handle is not reported through `XErrorReport`), and the text entry handle of the open text entry pull request does not report yet. The speech synthesizer, screenshot stream, sign-out deferral and store product query handles are never created here.
+* Handle tracking (`runtime/impl_handles.cpp`, tested by `test_misc.cpp`): the calls that hand out task queue, user, display timeout deferral, game save provider, container and update, package installation monitor, package mount, store context and store licence handles report them, so `XSystemIsHandleValid` is true from the first reference handed to the title until its last close, and the `XSystemHandleTrack` callback gets Created and Destroyed at those two points (duplicating a handle that returns the same pointer, as `XTaskQueueDuplicateHandle` and `XUserDuplicateHandle` do, adds a reference and no event). The callback runs synchronously on the thread that created or closed the handle; a null callback turns it off, and handles stay tracked for `XSystemIsHandleValid`. `UninitializeApiImpl` (`XGameRuntimeUninitialize`) reports each handle still open through `XErrorReport` with `E_FAIL` and a message naming its type; the code and text are not documented. Text entry handles from `XGameUiTextEntryOpen` report as GameUiTextEntry. Not done: handles passed into other calls are not validated (an invalid handle is not reported through `XErrorReport`). The speech synthesizer, screenshot stream, sign-out deferral and store product query handles are never created here.
+* Multiplayer activity invite and state share (`runtime/impl_system.cpp`, tested by `test_ui.cpp`): `XGameUiShowMultiplayerActivityGameInviteAsync` goes to the title's `showMultiplayerActivityGameInviteCallback` when one is set (answered with `XGameUiSetMultiplayerActivityGameInviteUiResponse`), otherwise it completes with `E_ABORT` like the other headless dialogs. `XGameUiUiCallbacks` has no state-share callback, so `XGameUiShowStateShareAsync` always completes with `E_ABORT`. Both reject a null async block or user with `E_INVALIDARG`, and state share also a null link token (the parameters are `_In_`; the pages name no code).
+* In-game text entry (`runtime/impl_system.cpp`, tested by `test_ui.cpp`; first written by Micolash21 in #63): no keyboard is shown. `XGameUiTextEntryOpen` returns a handle that keeps the initial text (empty when none) and the cursor, clamped to the text; `maxLength` is not checked, since nothing is typed. The first `XGameUiTextEntryGetState` reports `Dismissed` with that text, later calls report `None`; the IME clause is empty at the cursor. `GetExtents` is an all-zero rectangle (nothing on screen). A buffer without room for the text and its terminator gets `HRESULT_FROM_WIN32(ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER)`. Unknown position hints or visibility flags, null options, and closed or unknown handles get `E_INVALIDARG`; the console's codes for those cases are not verified. The handle is reported to `XSystemHandleTrack` and `XSystemIsHandleValid` as GameUiTextEntry from open to close.
 * Launching (`runtime/impl_launch.cpp`, tested by `test_launch.cpp`): `XLaunchUri` drops a leading `ms-bgm-` (the documented PC behaviour) and opens the URI with `ShellExecuteExW`, waiting until the shell has launched it, so Windows shows its own prompt when no app handles the scheme. It returns `E_INVALIDARG` for text without a scheme of at least two characters (so a drive path such as `C:\game.exe` is never run) or that is not UTF-8, and `E_ACCESSDENIED` for `file:` URIs; both refusals are local choices. The requesting user is ignored. `XLaunchNewGame` starts the executable (relative paths resolve against the current directory) with the given arguments and the caller's current directory, then ends the calling process with `TerminateProcess` and exit code 0, also when the new game could not be started (documented). Both processes exist for a moment, so the one-executable-at-a-time guarantee holds only once the caller has exited; DLC is not unmounted and the default user is ignored (there is one local user). `XLaunchRestartOnCrash` rejects a non-zero `reserved` with `E_INVALIDARG` and registers the arguments with `RegisterApplicationRestart` for crashes only (no restart for hangs, updates or reboots); after such a crash Windows Error Reporting offers the user the chance to restart the game and never restarts it without the user's consent, and only for a process that ran for at least 60 seconds (both from the `RegisterApplicationRestart` page), and the console's devkit-only `E_NOTIMPL` does not apply on PC.
 * Game UI drawn by the title (`runtime/impl_ui.cpp`, tested by `test_ui.cpp`): after `XGameUiSetUiCallbacks`, each of the message dialog, error dialog, player picker, profile card, send-invite, achievements and text entry calls whose callback is set calls it on its async block's queue (the process queue for a block without one) when the async work runs, with a callback handle, copies of the call's strings and arrays, and the title's context; the call stays pending until the matching `XGameUiSet*UiResponse`, whose values the Result calls then return. `useSystemUiIfAvailable` makes no difference because no system UI exists here. A null `callbacks` pointer clears them; a null member leaves that UI headless (`E_ABORT`). A response call returns `E_INVALIDARG` for a handle that no callback was given, that belongs to another kind of UI, that was already answered or whose call was canceled; `XAsyncCancel` ends a pending call with `E_ABORT`. `XGameUiShowTextEntryResultSize` counts the terminator of the title's text; the response is not checked against `maxTextLength`, and the player picker response is not checked against the selection limits. `XGameUiSetMultiplayerActivityGameInviteUiResponse` refuses every handle until the multiplayer activity invite UI (in an open pull request) routes to its callback.
   `XGameUiShowWebAuthentication(WithOptions)Async` check the user, an `https` request URI, the completion URI and the options (PreferFullscreen is ignored on PC, documented), then complete with `E_ABORT` like the other headless UI: no web view is shown and nothing is fetched. ResultSize and Result return that error and leave the result pointer null.
